  1890	The binding digest is an opaque audit reference to a host-local Terminal
  1891	Instance Binding; the binding object itself is not an event member and is not
  1892	replicated. The evidence IDs MUST resolve locally to validated, sanitized
  1893	Manifest, Probe, and applicable Capability Evidence objects bound to the event's
  1894	backend ID and versions. They MUST NOT resolve through the event to a native
  1895	reference, generation string, socket, pipe, endpoint, token, credential,
  1896	terminal output, PID/handle, or live-process fact.
  1897	
  1898	<code>terminal.created</code> records the backend selected for a new AX-
  1899	authorized activation. <code>session.resumed</code> records the backend used by
  1900	that owner resume. A valid takeover or owner resume MAY select a different
  1901	admitted destination backend and emits a new binding digest and v4 event; it
  1902	does not clone/fork the LogicalSession and never mutates an earlier event.
  1903	Attach or presentation-client activity emits neither event and cannot change
  1904	Owner/Replica, lease, or fencing state. A reader of v1-v3 may retain a v4 event
  1905	as inert immutable history but MUST NOT derive runtime state or write a lower-
  1906	version replacement.
  1907	
  1908	### 5.3 Lease Record and ownership
  1909	
  1910	The Lease Record schema is <code>urn:ax:schema:lease</code> version
  1911	<code>1.0.0</code>. Fields are:
  1912	
  1913	| Field | Type | Constraint |
  1914	| --- | --- | --- |
  1915	| <code>schema</code> | string | Exact Lease Record schema identifier |
  1916	| <code>schema_version</code> | semver | Exact <code>1.0.0</code> |
  1917	| <code>record_id</code> | digest | Canonical Lease Record digest |
  1918	| <code>subject_id</code> | UUIDv7 | Equal to <code>session_id</code> |
  1919	| <code>lease_id</code> | UUIDv4 | Cryptographically random unique fencing token |
  1920	| <code>session_id</code> | UUIDv7 | Lease scope |
  1921	| <code>epoch</code> | uint53 | Starts at 1; never decreases |
  1922	| <code>holder_host_id</code> | UUIDv7 | Proposed owner |
  1923	| <code>predecessor_lease_id</code> | UUIDv4 or null | Null only at epoch 1 |
  1924	| <code>reason</code> | enum | <code>create</code>, <code>graceful_takeover</code>, <code>force_takeover</code>, <code>recovery</code> |
  1925	| <code>checkpoint_id</code> | digest or null | Null only for epoch-1 <code>create</code>; otherwise the validated materialized handoff base |
  1926	| <code>issued_by_host_id</code> | UUIDv7 | Initiator |
  1927	| <code>created_by_host_id</code> | UUIDv7 | MUST equal <code>issued_by_host_id</code> |
  1928	| <code>created_at</code> | timestamp | Diagnostic only |
  1929	| <code>extensions</code> | object | Reverse-DNS extension keys only |
  1930	
  1931	The object is closed; every row is required, including nullable fields.
  1932	Normative example:
  1933	
  1934	~~~json
  1935	{
  1936	  "schema": "urn:ax:schema:lease",
  1937	  "schema_version": "1.0.0",
  1938	  "record_id": "sha256:8ead987abed8c7c05175b447c9a0e2b3a521f12e5a989acb8abe132576852d63",
  1939	  "subject_id": "0198f4c8-3e70-7a11-8a2b-1234567890ab",
  1940	  "session_id": "0198f4c8-3e70-7a11-8a2b-1234567890ab",
  1941	  "lease_id": "aaaaaaaa-bbbb-4ccc-8ddd-eeeeeeeeeeee",
  1942	  "epoch": 4,
  1943	  "holder_host_id": "0198f4c8-7d40-7e55-8e6f-1234567890ab",
  1944	  "predecessor_lease_id": "bbbbbbbb-cccc-4ddd-8eee-ffffffffffff",
  1945	  "reason": "graceful_takeover",
  1946	  "checkpoint_id": "sha256:e051996f51f13ace4f5cdebe1e30fd26fd5fe104cfd6e6a7f9f1206ba3819656",
  1947	  "issued_by_host_id": "0198f4c8-7d40-7e55-8e6f-1234567890ab",
  1948	  "created_by_host_id": "0198f4c8-7d40-7e55-8e6f-1234567890ab",
  1949	  "created_at": "2026-08-19T04:09:00.000Z",
  1950	  "extensions": {}
  1951	}
  1952	~~~
  1953	
  1954	The winning lease is selected by the greatest tuple
  1955	<code>(epoch, lease_id)</code>, where <code>lease_id</code> uses bytewise UUID
  1956	order. Lease UUIDs are random UUIDv4 values, so wall-clock skew cannot bias the
  1957	tie-break. A valid epoch greater than 1 MUST name a known predecessor for the
  1958	same session, MUST equal that predecessor's epoch plus one, and MUST reference
  1959	a validated checkpoint for that session and predecessor lease. An epoch-1
  1960	<code>create</code> lease MUST have a null predecessor and MAY have a null
  1961	checkpoint before the first provider boundary exists. Every new takeover lease
  1962	MUST use <code>max_observed_epoch + 1</code> from the initiator's current union.
  1963	
  1964	The tuple rule handles concurrent force takeovers under partition without
  1965	clock ordering. Both processes can temporarily continue in isolated partitions,
  1966	which is why force takeover warns about split brain. After union, exactly one
  1967	lease wins. Events under the losing same-epoch lease and all lower epochs MUST
  1968	be preserved in a divergent branch and MUST NOT affect authoritative state.
  1969	
  1970	An owner process MUST revalidate its fencing token before:
  1971	
  1972	- accepting operator input after attach;
  1973	- starting a provider turn;
  1974	- publishing a checkpoint;
  1975	- pushing records or workspace changes; and
  1976	- resuming after any transport or sleep interruption longer than the configured
  1977	  lease refresh interval.
  1978	
  1979	There is no time-expiring ownership lease in v0.5.0. Liveness is not authority.
  1980	A host being offline does not make a replica owner; only a takeover or fork
  1981	does.
  1982	
  1983	### 5.4 Checkpoint Record
  1984	
  1985	The Checkpoint Record schema is <code>urn:ax:schema:checkpoint</code> version
  1986	<code>1.0.0</code>. Its top-level object is closed and contains exactly:
  1987	
  1988	| Field | Type | Constraint |
  1989	| --- | --- | --- |
  1990	| <code>schema</code> | string | Exact Checkpoint schema identifier |
  1991	| <code>schema_version</code> | semver | Exact <code>1.0.0</code> |
  1992	| <code>checkpoint_id</code> | digest | Canonical object digest |
  1993	| <code>subject_id</code> | UUIDv7 | Equal to <code>session_id</code> |
  1994	| <code>session_id</code> | UUIDv7 | Existing Session Record |
  1995	| <code>lease_epoch</code> | uint53 | Greater than zero and equal to the referenced winning lease |
  1996	| <code>lease_id</code> | UUIDv4 | Equal to that lease's fencing token |
  1997	| <code>safe_boundary</code> | Safe Boundary Evidence | Closed shape below |
  1998	| <code>event_heads</code> | sorted unique digest[1..64] | Authoritative event DAG heads immediately before this object |
  1999	| <code>workspace_manifest_id</code> | digest | Workspace-group Transfer Manifest root |
  2000	| <code>provider_manifest_id</code> | digest or null | Direct native-store/provider snapshot only |
  2001	| <code>task_board_bundle_id</code> | digest or null | Task-board path only |
  2002	| <code>created_by_host_id</code> | UUIDv7 | Current lease holder |
  2003	| <code>created_at</code> | timestamp | Diagnostic only |
  2004	| <code>status</code> | enum | Literal <code>validated</code> |
  2005	| <code>extensions</code> | object | Reverse-DNS extension keys only |
  2006	
  2007	Safe Boundary Evidence is a closed object containing exactly
  2008	<code>provider_id:provider-id</code>,
  2009	<code>provider_version:string[1..128]</code>,
  2010	<code>evidence:provider_api|provider_event|managed_pty|task_board_bridge|accepted_test</code>,
  2011	<code>input_blocked:boolean</code>, <code>foreground_idle:boolean</code>,
  2012	<code>background_idle:boolean</code>, <code>open_processes:uint53</code>, and
  2013	<code>open_database_handles:uint53</code>. A published checkpoint requires all
  2014	three booleans true and both counters zero. Provider-specific scheduled or
  2015	child work is included in <code>background_idle</code> and
  2016	<code>open_processes</code>; an adapter that cannot prove it MUST NOT publish a
  2017	new checkpoint.
  2018	
  2019	The referenced Session Record selects the persistence variant. For
  2020	<code>kind = direct</code>, <code>provider_manifest_id</code> MUST be non-null
  2021	and <code>task_board_bundle_id</code> MUST be null. A backend-resolved provider
  2022	with no portable bytes still uses a provider Transfer Manifest containing its
  2023	validated identity and zero file entries; null is not used to erase that
  2024	distinction. For <code>kind = task_board</code>,
  2025	<code>provider_manifest_id</code> MUST be null and
  2026	<code>task_board_bundle_id</code> MUST be non-null. Both-null and both-non-null
  2027	checkpoints are invalid.
  2028	
  2029	A checkpoint MUST NOT be published until every referenced object exists,
  2030	hashes correctly, passes schema validation, and the provider/task-board path
  2031	meets its quiescence requirement. A force takeover MAY select the newest
  2032	validated checkpoint even when no new safe checkpoint can be created.
  2033	
  2034	<code>event_heads</code> identifies the authoritative event DAG immediately
  2035	before the checkpoint object. A later <code>checkpoint.created</code> event MAY
  2036	reference that checkpoint and those heads; the checkpoint MUST NOT reference
  2037	the announcing event because that would create a circular digest dependency.
  2038	The transitive event-head closure also fixes the Section 2.4 effective
  2039	execution profile and its nullable source event for this checkpoint. A missing,
  2040	losing-lease, or non-newest <code>profile.changed</code> reference is
  2041	<code>integrity_failure</code>; checkpoint consumers MUST NOT consult a later
  2042	local-only profile event or fall back to the creation profile.
  2043	
  2044	Normative negative fixtures are: <code>CP-N1</code>, with
  2045	<code>background_idle = false</code> and status <code>validated</code>;

diff --git a/internal/sessquery/rev14_regression_test.go b/internal/sessquery/rev14_regression_test.go
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..1fde8c22fd6361988fbb9e733929be1feb6eb964
--- /dev/null
+++ b/internal/sessquery/rev14_regression_test.go
@@ -0,0 +1,160 @@
+package sessquery
+
+import (
+	"errors"
+	"fmt"
+	"os"
+	"path/filepath"
+	"runtime"
+	"testing"
+
+	"github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager/internal/sessrepo"
+)
+
+// TestRev14CapabilitySeal proves that the profile derivation boundary does
+// not treat a zero or field-assembled admittedCheckpoint as authority. The
+// product entry points are intentionally exercised directly here because a
+// missing seal must fail before any repository or event read can occur.
+func TestRev14CapabilitySeal(t *testing.T) {
+	recordBytes := record(t, idA, "alpha")
+	forged := admittedCheckpoint{
+		seal: &checkpointSeal{
+			Digest:              "sha256:1111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111111",
+			SessionID:           idA,
+			LeaseEpoch:          1,
+			LeaseID:             lease,
+			CreatorHostID:       hostA,
+			HasProviderManifest: true,
+			EventHeads:          []string{zeroDigest},
+		},
+	}
+	entries := map[string]func(admittedCheckpoint) error{
+		"checkCheckpointProfileAuthority": func(checkpoint admittedCheckpoint) error {
+			return checkCheckpointProfileAuthority(checkpoint, nil, nil)
+		},
+		"sessionCreationProfile": func(checkpoint admittedCheckpoint) error {
+			_, _, err := sessionCreationProfile(checkpoint, recordBytes)
+			return err
+		},
+		"profileClosure": func(checkpoint admittedCheckpoint) error {
+			_, err := profileClosure(checkpoint, zeroDigest, nil)
+			return err
+		},
+		"eventProfilePair": func(checkpoint admittedCheckpoint) error {
+			_, err := eventProfilePair(checkpoint, nil, sessrepo.EventSummary{})
+			return err
+		},
+		"eventProfileTarget": func(checkpoint admittedCheckpoint) error {
+			_, err := eventProfileTarget(checkpoint, nil, sessrepo.EventSummary{})
+			return err
+		},
+		"eventPayloadMembers": func(checkpoint admittedCheckpoint) error {
+			_, err := eventPayloadMembers(checkpoint, nil, sessrepo.EventSummary{})
+			return err
+		},
+		"checkClosureProfilePairs": func(checkpoint admittedCheckpoint) error {
+			return checkClosureProfilePairs(checkpoint, "standard", zeroDigest, nil, nil, nil, nil)
+		},
+		"referencedCheckpointProfile": func(checkpoint admittedCheckpoint) error {
+			_, _, _, _, err := referencedCheckpointProfile(checkpoint, sessrepo.EventSummary{}, "standard", zeroDigest, nil, nil, nil)
+			return err
+		},
+		"checkProfilePairSource": func(checkpoint admittedCheckpoint) error {
+			return checkProfilePairSource(checkpoint, sessrepo.EventSummary{}, profilePair{}, "standard", "", false, nil, nil)
+		},
+	}
+	for name, entry := range entries {
+		for label, checkpoint := range map[string]admittedCheckpoint{
+			"zero":   {},
+			"forged": forged,
+		} {
+			t.Run(fmt.Sprintf("%s/%s", name, label), func(t *testing.T) {
+				err := entry(checkpoint)
+				if err == nil {
+					t.Fatalf("%s accepted an unsealed checkpoint", label)
+				}
+				if !errors.Is(err, ErrObservationUnavailable) {
+					t.Fatalf("%s refusal = %v, want observation_unavailable", label, err)
+				}
+			})
+		}
+	}
+}
+
+// TestRev14RecordConsumptionCensusRejectsAlternatePaths expands the
+// package-wide type gate with plants that preserve reviewer-visible tokens
+// while changing the authority object or construction path. A plant is
+// accepted only if the same production census rejects it.
+func TestRev14RecordConsumptionCensusRejectsAlternatePaths(t *testing.T) {
+	_, file, _, ok := runtime.Caller(0)
+	if !ok {
+		t.Fatal("cannot locate test file")
+	}
+	packageDir := filepath.Dir(file)
+	plants := map[string]string{
+		"interface_assertion.go": `package sessquery
+
+func review14Interface(v any) string { return v.(validatedCheckpoint).Digest }
+`,
+		"package_closure.go": `package sessquery
+
+var review14Closure = func(v validatedCheckpoint) string { return v.Digest }
+`,
+		"unresolvable_callee.go": `package sessquery
+
+func review14Unresolved() { missingReview14Callee() }
+`,
+		"method_named_admit.go": `package sessquery
+
+type review14Receiver struct{}
+
+func (review14Receiver) admitCheckpoint(v validatedCheckpoint) (admittedCheckpoint, error) {
+		return admittedCheckpoint{}, nil
+}
+`,
+		"field_assembly.go": `package sessquery
+
+func review14FieldAssembly(raw validatedCheckpoint) admittedCheckpoint {
+		var checkpoint admittedCheckpoint
+		checkpoint.seal = &checkpointSeal{Digest: raw.Digest, SessionID: raw.SessionID}
+		return checkpoint
+}
+`,
+		"embedding.go": `package sessquery
+
+type review14Embedding struct{ admittedCheckpoint }
+`,
+		"raw_copy.go": `package sessquery
+
+func review14RawCopy(raw validatedCheckpoint) admittedCheckpoint {
+		var checkpoint admittedCheckpoint
+		checkpoint.seal = &checkpointSeal{Digest: raw.Digest, SessionID: raw.SessionID}
+		return checkpoint
+}
+`,
+		"capability_map.go": `package sessquery
+
+var review14Capabilities = map[string]admittedCheckpoint{}
+
+func review14Populate(raw validatedCheckpoint) {
+		var checkpoint admittedCheckpoint
+		checkpoint.seal = &checkpointSeal{Digest: raw.Digest, SessionID: raw.SessionID}
+		review14Capabilities[raw.Digest] = checkpoint
+}
+`,
+	}
+	for name, plant := range plants {
+		t.Run(name, func(t *testing.T) {
+			dir := t.TempDir()
+			rev12CopyProductionSources(t, packageDir, dir)
+			if err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(dir, name), []byte(plant), 0o600); err != nil {
+				t.Fatal(err)
+			}
+			if err := rev12CheckRecordConsumptionSource(dir); err == nil {
+				t.Fatalf("record-consumption gate admitted %s", name)
+			} else {
+				t.Logf("rejected %s: %v", name, err)
+			}
+		})
+	}
+}

